## Coverage is preserved, and that is measured

The deleted test file reported **29 passed**. Section A (11 cases, the close
wrapper) died with its subject. Sections B and C (18 cases, the pre-push hook)
were extracted to `scripts/pre-push_test.sh`.

11 + 18 = 29. The parity is exact, so nothing was silently dropped — including
C1, which an earlier draft of the plan would have deleted while every stated
check stayed green.

The extracted suite now reports **19 passed, 0 failed**: the 18 carried cases
plus one added at the verify gate's suggestion, pinning that the hook's BLOCKED
message names the current `etude capture` / `etude gate` flow. That assertion is
the only thing standing between a future edit and another dangling instruction
of exactly the kind this bead spent ten sweep sites removing. Negative control:
reverting the message to generic wording fails it (18 passed, 1 failed);
restoring returns 19/19.

## Negative controls

**Hook wiring.** The suite must fail when the hook it targets is broken.
Removing `_has_code_ref=true` from the `refs/heads/*` classification: **5 of 18
fail, exit 1.** Proves `$PRE_PUSH_HOOK` resolves to the real hook under change.

The plan's originally specified control — breaking the `refs/etude/*` exemption —
did NOT fail, and the diagnosis is recorded rather than the control quietly
swapped: with that arm removed, such a ref falls to the `*)` branch, which sets
`_all_exempt=false` but leaves `_has_code_ref` false, and the next guard exits 0
when no code ref is present. The breakage is masked downstream, so B1's
assertions hold either way. A control has to target a branch whose breakage is
observable.

**bd shim.** Removing the `export PATH` line left 18/18 green, because this
machine's real `bd` behaves compatibly — so removal cannot demonstrate anything
here. Replacing the shim with a `bd` that exits 7: **13 of 18 fail.** The suite
genuinely depends on the shim to be insulated from whatever `bd` a machine
carries; that is why it was on the carry list.

All three mutations reverted; both files confirmed byte-identical afterwards.

## The live hook

`.beads/hooks/pre-push` is edited and is on the path of every push in this
checkout, so the blast radius exceeds this bead.

- The diff touches two strings: a comment and the BLOCKED message. Filtering the
  diff for `if`/`exit`/`case`/`while`/test constructs returns nothing.
- `bash -n` clean.
- **Exercised by the extracted suite, NOT by any push.** `scripts/pre-push_test.sh`
  resolves `$PRE_PUSH_HOOK` through this worktree's own `REPO_ROOT`, so all 19
  cases run against the edited copy — including B3, which executes the reworded
  BLOCKED branch and now asserts on the new wording.

  **Correction.** An earlier version of this artifact claimed the edited hook had
  been "exercised for real" by every push since the edit. That was false, and the
  verify gate blocked on it. `git config --show-origin core.hooksPath` resolves
  to the MAIN checkout's `.beads/hooks`, and there is no
  `extensions.worktreeConfig` override, so pushes from this worktree execute
  `/Users/jv/projects/etude/.beads/hooks/pre-push` — which `diff` confirms is
  still the UNEDITED version. No push has run the edited hook, and none will
  until this lands on main and that checkout's copy is updated. The claim was
  also self-contradicting: `scripts/pre-push_test.sh` in the same diff already
  says `core.hooksPath` points at the main checkout.

  This cuts both ways, and the second half is the reassuring one: a syntax error
  in this edit could not have broken other lanes' pushes either, because the file
  is not the executing hook from here.

## Known gaps, stated rather than papered over

- **`make dogfood-audit` does not pass**, on `cadence-sidecar` retro-meta sidecar
  debt for the `etude-9uf.2` and `etude-rep` cohorts, plus — transiently — an
  `unpushed-ref` gap for this bead's own run ref whenever a stage or gate has
  been captured since the last push. None of it is caused by this diff, and bead
